{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"electrical resistance electrical resistance","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md","fileName":"Electrical Resistance.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Electrical Resistance","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-17 00:04\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Electrostatics and DC Circuits]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Electrostatics and DC Circuits","foundationsofphysics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"electrical resistance electrical resistance","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md","fileName":"Electrical Resistance.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Electrical Resistance","heading":"Electrical Resistance","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Electrical resistance is the ratio $R=V/I$ for a component at a specified operating condition. An ohmic conductor has a linear current-voltage relation with constant resistance. For a uniform wire, $R=\\rho L/A$, linking resistance to material resistivity, length, and cross-sectional area. Temperature can change resistivity and invalidate a constant-$R$ approximation.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Electrostatics and DC Circuits","foundationsofphysics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"electrical resistance electrical resistance","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Electrical Resistance.md","fileName":"Electrical Resistan